      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "for each Loop"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The for-each loop makes accessing array elements so easy."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "content": "1class Main {\n2    public static void main(String[] args) {\n3        \n4        // Create an array\n5        int[] primeNumbers = {2, 3, 5, 7};\n6        \n7        System.out.println(\"Prime Numbers:\");\n8        \n9        for (int number : primeNumbers) {\n10            System.out.println(number);\n11        }\n12    }\n13}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, in each iteration of the for-each loop, an element of the primeNumbers array is stored in the number variable. This is why the array elements are printed when we print number. Now let us look at some examples that involve arrays."
    },
